cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: preprint
title: "Dynamic Longest Palindromic Substring via Mergeable Summaries"
version: "5.1"
authors:
  - family-names: "Liu"
    given-names: "Chengde"
date-released: "2026-11-22"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
abstract: >-
  We give a data structure maintaining the longest palindromic substring of a
  string under character replacement, insertion, deletion, range reversal and
  range cyclic shift, answering the query on an arbitrary interval. Each node
  of an implicit Treap stores handles of its substring and of the reverse of
  its substring, which turns range reversal into a lazy flag and requires no
  reversal primitive from the underlying dynamic string structure. Using the
  persistent dynamic string collection of Gawrychowski, Karczmarz, Kociumaka,
  Łącki and Sankowski as a black box, every operation runs in O(log^3 N) time,
  simultaneously over a polynomially long execution with high probability,
  against an oblivious adversary, and answers are exact. On the high-probability
  event, each operation increases the space by O(log^3 N) words, so q operations
  use O(N + n_0 log^2 N + q log^3 N) words. With amortised time we also prove a
  q-independent bound: rebuilding every Theta(N/log N) operations gives
  O(N log^2 N) words and O(log^3 N) amortised time per operation, assuming
  discarded instances are deallocated. The stronger O(N log N) and O(N) variants
  would require handle-free summaries with update rollback, which the black box
  does not provide; they are not claimed.
keywords:
  - dynamic strings
  - longest palindromic substring
  - Treap
  - arithmetic progressions
  - LCE
  - data structures
  - range reversal
